RM                                                                    RM

NAME
    rm - delete files and directories, Unix-style

FORMAT
    rm [-rfv] FILE | PATTERN ...

TEMPLATE
    none - flags are hand-parsed Unix bundles; `rm ?` prints usage

PATH
    C:rm

FUNCTION
    Deletes files, pattern matches, and (with -r) whole directory
    trees. C:Delete exists; rm is the muscle memory - with this
    family's manners and its safety model:

    A directory is refused without -r, like Unix (and unlike
    Delete ALL's promptless enthusiasm). With -r the tree is walked
    with an explicit work list: every directory is scanned to
    completion BEFORE anything inside it is deleted, files go as
    they are met, and the directories themselves are removed
    deepest-first once their contents are gone. A child that
    refuses to go leaves its whole parent chain standing, each
    parent reporting "not empty" honestly.

    A delete-protected file (the d bit - the Amiga's own safety
    catch) is SKIPPED by default and listed under "not deleted:".
    A file another task holds open always refuses.

    VOLUME ROOTS ARE REFUSED UNCONDITIONALLY. `rm -rf dh0:` answers
    "refusing to remove a volume root" and does nothing else; no
    flag overrides it. Format and Delete ALL exist for the one day
    a decade that is really meant.

    Links - soft links, and hard links to directories - are
    deleted as LINKS, never followed: the entry goes, the target
    stays, and a linked ancestor can never turn -r into a cycle.

OPTIONS
    -r  delete directories and their contents (also accepted: -R)
    -f  force, the Amiga way: strip the delete protection and
        retry; also silences "no match" for arguments that match
        nothing
    -v  list each object as it is deleted (default silent)

RESULTS
    Return code 0 when clean, 5 when something was skipped (and
    listed under "not deleted:"), 10 when something failed, 20 on
    Ctrl-C, which is honoured between entries.

EXAMPLES
    rm old.txt
    rm #?.bak
    rm -r work:tmpdir
    rm -f locked.dat
    rm -rv ram:t

SEE ALSO
    ls.doc, cp.doc, mv.doc, mkdir.doc, tools-audit.md
